  "client_view_text": " In this way, a write-ahead log can prevent the loss of data that would be lost in the event of a crash.\nWhen a database updates the data, it first writes the changes to the write-ahead log. The write-ahead log is a record of all the changes that have been made to the data, in the order they were made. This means that even if the database crashes before it has a chance to update the data in memory, the write-ahead log still contains a record of the changes that need to be made. After the crash, the database can recover by re-applying the changes recorded in the write-ahead log to the data. This ensures that the data is consistent and up-to-date, even if the database crashed.\nThe write-ahead log serves as a safety net that prevents data loss in the event of a crash. By writing the changes to the log before updating the data, the database ensures that it always has a record of the changes that need to be made, even if the data itself is lost. This makes it possible for the database to recover and restore the data to a consistent state, even if the database crashed.\nIn addition to preventing data loss, the write-ahead log also helps to ensure that the database can recover to a consistent state, even"
